00:00:02.659 --> 00:00:04.610 align:start position:0%
I'm joined today by dr. Jeff Szymanski  who's currently the executive director  of the international OCD foundation jeff  was previously the director of  psychological services at McLean  Hospitals OCD Institute he's also author  of the book the perfectionists handbook  and serves as a Clinical Instructor in  psychology at Harvard Medical School so  Jeff let's start sort of at the top when  we talk about OCD obsessive compulsive  disorder there's a lot of sort of  laypersons terms that are thrown around  about what that is and what the symptoms  are but what are we talking about when  we talk about OCD yeah so in the way  that you just said the best place to  start is to define some of those terms  because the way people use for example  obsession and late conversation is  different than how you sit in in  psychology and psychiatry so obsessive  in general conversation means I'm  obsessed with my favorite baseball team  I'm obsessed with the color purple  meaning I really like something that's  how we use it when we use the word  compulsive in everyday language we're  talking about people that are neat  people that are wanted people that are  very detail-oriented  people that are perfectionistic right so  the problem with those two terms in lay  language having the same words in the  title of OCD is that everyone gets them  confused right so they say oh I'm a  little OCD because I'm obsessed with  having my sock drawer compulsively  arranged and so what happens for people  with OCD is they hear that they're like  oh you just don't really understand or  get my experience because in the context  of obsessive compulsive disorder  obsession means unwanted intrusive  thoughts and images things I don't want  to be thinking about things I don't want  to be seeing in my mind and they create  a lot of unwanted feelings primarily  anxiety but also guilt disgust other  kinds of feelings that a person with OCD  doesn't want to have so these these  obsessions actually turn up in a lot of  frequency and a lot of intensity and so  as the anxiety increases and the  unwanted feelings the unwanted thoughts  increase the person feels compelled to  engage in compulsive behavior now the  compulsive behaviors are repeated  behaviors you're doing internally so it  could be counting or praying or saying a  good word over and over again in order  to keep yourself safe but more could be  external behavior so a lot of people  have seen the cleaning and and and hand  washing etc but there's a lot of  different kinds of compulsions that  could range from having to do things in  certain number of times or also just  kind of avoiding certain situations that  would evoke the obsessions in the  anxiety so what so the difference  between someone being obsessed with  their baseball team and someone having  OCD is that someone with OCD actually is  suffering on a day-to-day basis and the  anxiety in the obsessions and the  compulsive behavior are time consuming  and they're getting in the way of  day-to-day activities so people are  showing up late to work or they can't  work they can't get their schoolwork  done they're not being able to see  family members or friends that they'd  like be able to be able to see so again  the D and OC d stands for disorder  meaning that the person's day-to-day  functioning is is significantly impaired  how prevalent is OCD in in the sense  that you're describing it in American  society world I mean do we have probably  the world is tough because there's  there's so many countries where we just  really wouldn't have data but maybe in  the u.s. so in the u.s. this has been  shown several times that at any given  point about 1% of the adult population  has OCD and which translates into about  two to three million adults right now  would qualify for an OC diagnosis  another half million to a million kids  would also qualify for an OCD diagnosis  in fact we do have a number of studies  worldwide and that same prevalence of  OCD stays consistent across country that  it's about 1% of the population now  that's interesting where you say it's  consistent across countries because one  of the things I wanted to discuss was  whether OCD is a sort of modern West  condition in the sense that if we were  to go back a hundred thousand years or  something like that in human history do  we know anything about the prevalence of  OCD or something like it or is it really  something that has been sort of created  or a result of modern westernization so  we know from neuroimaging studies  actually that there are some brain  abnormalities for people that have OCD  we know from genetics research that  about one in four kids that have a  parent with OCD will have OCD so there's  a genetic transmission there's a  neurobiological component to this the  way that people have talked about OCD  and other mental illness um historically  is looking at autobiographies that  people wrote or biographies where people  had written about him Vincent van Gogh I  think was a example of someone who  likely suffered from bipolar disorder so  the idea that it that it's a a modern  kind of a modern manifestation of  something about our society probably not  so much I think mental illness has been  around for quite a while and you know to  this day what we still hear people say  is I had OCD for many years I just  didn't have a name for it I didn't know  what it was so I think that that was the  case historically is that someone was  behaving in an odd way  I mean reporting being very distressed  and they just didn't have a name for it  you talked about some of the examples of  what our actual OCD symptoms are there  examples of symptoms attributed to OCD  that aren't really OCD symptoms I think  it probably it probably goes in the  other direction where what what the  number of people in our community find  is that they were misdiagnosed with  something else rather than OCD so for  example people some people with OCD will  have violent thoughts and there are  thoughts that they don't want to have it  isn't I had a thought of harming someone  and I think I might do it it's the exact  opposite there they're so concerned that  they had the thought that they now start  policing their mind oh my goodness of  have this thought again this is a  terrible thing I'm a terrible person and  then they start engaging in all this  compulsive behavior to make their mind  not think these thoughts again you're  not a violent person they've never  committed any violence in the past and  so what happens is that they they're  they're compulsive behavior increases  they go and report this to a mental  health professional who may not know  about this form of OCD and they start to  think of them as a potential violent  risk same thing with sexual thoughts  there are some people that have unwanted  sexual thoughts and if they go to their  mental health professional who doesn't  know anything about OCD they might think  oh maybe this person has some sort of  sexual disorder or maybe they're at risk  for harming other people and so we don't  have so much other things get confused  with those idiots that OCD actually gets  under not thought of and misdiagnosed  quite frequently a lot of folks that I  work with would be diagnosed with  psychotic illness like schizophrenia  because sometimes the thoughts can be  the obsessions can be very bizarre but  the person knows that the thoughts are  bizarre and that ends up being one of  the primary differences what constitutes  treatment for OCD can it be treated  effectively is our our pharmaceuticals  the answer is is it something else so  unfortunately the research recent  research is showing it takes somewhere  between 14 to 17 years between onset of  OCD symptoms and actually accessing  effective treatment and the problem one  of the many problems with that is that  there is effective treatment and so  there's a class of medications called  selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors  more commonly thought of as  antidepressants but they have anti OCD  properties as well and so for many  people with mild to moderate OCD they go  to the psychiatrist or primary care  physician if they've been diagnosed with  OCD and they get on an SSRI that might  actually be the treatment of choice for  them there's lots of reasons for showing  that medicating these medications can be  helpful for OCD however the primary  treatment that we work for  two is called exposure and response  prevention therapy and I liken this to  if you know someone who's afraid of dogs  what's the best way to get someone over  their fear of dogs it's you have to  expose them to being around dogs so it's  basically the same kind of an  oversimplified fashion that's basically  the same idea with OCD is that we  actually have the person think the  purposely think about the obsessions and  put themselves in contact with  situations that would evoke anxiety and  then we ask them to not engage in the  compulsive behavior and so when you  break that link of having anxiety and  then responding to it with compulsive  behavior and you now kind of cut that  off what happens is the person will  continue to experience the anxiety and  what your brain does then it says oh  wait a minute this thing that I  categorized as possibly dangerous maybe  isn't as dangerous as I thought it was  and so then your brain starts to  decrease its anxiety and so we called  this habituation and so you get used to  being in a certain situation and over  time if people use exposure response  prevention therapy they expose  themselves to anxiety and situations  that evoke their obsessions they don't  engage in the response prevention as  they don't engage in the compulsive  behavior what they find is that their  anxiety over time decreases and they can  approach more situations absolutely  fascinating the book is the  perfectionists handbook we've been  speaking with the book's author dr. Jeff  Szymanski who is currently the executive  director of the international OCD  foundation thanks so much for talking to  us about this no problem thank you
 

